<h2>The eviction case</h2>
<p>The landlords sought eviction of a commercial shop of about 14.36 square yards at Abdul Rehman Road, Karol Bagh, under Section 14(1)(e) read with Section 25-B of the Delhi Rent Control Act, pleading bona fide need to expand their business — more space for storage and operations. They relied on a site plan of the property. The tenant obtained leave to defend, and the matter went to evidence before the Additional Rent Controller, who allowed the eviction petition in March 2025, holding the bona fide requirement established. The tenant — represented after his death by legal representatives — carried the matter to the High Court in revision.</p>

<h2>The overlooked cross-examination</h2>
<p>The pivot of the revision was the landlords' own witness. In cross-examination, PW-1 said the tenanted premises were a single hall without partition, that there was no room or kitchen in his occupation, and — critically — that he could not say whether the site plan placed on record was correct. If the landlords' existing accommodation was different from what the plan showed, the finding that they lacked reasonably suitable alternative space stood on shaky ground.</p>
<p>The Rent Controller had noticed the site-plan arguments, but, the High Court held, never examined the cross-examination against the landlords' claim of inadequate accommodation. That was a material omission. Identifying the tenanted portion did not answer the distinct question of what accommodation the landlords already had, and the tenant's identification of his own shop was no admission that the whole plan was accurate.</p>

<h2>Supervisory jurisdiction, correctly used</h2>
<p>The judgment is equally careful about the limits of revision. Following <em>Hindustan Petroleum Corporation Ltd. v. Dilbahar Singh</em>, (2014) 9 SCC 78, and <em>Abid-ul-Islam v. Inder Sain Dua</em>, (2022) 6 SCC 30, the Court reiterated that the proviso to Section 25-B(8) creates a supervisory check, not a rehearing: evidence cannot be re-appreciated as a first appellate court would. Interference is confined to findings that rest on no evidence, on misreading, or on a failure to consider material evidence. This case fell in the last category, and the Court found support in a closely analogous remand in <em>Tek Chand v. Meenakshi Gupta</em>, 2025 SCC OnLine Del 6417. Precedents where tenants had raised only bald challenges to site plans — <em>Neelam Jain</em> and <em>Rajender Kumar Pahuja</em> — were distinguished, because here the challenge emerged from the landlords' own witness.</p>
