<h2>Takeaways for landlords and tenants</h2>
<p>For landlords, the case is a reminder that the site plan is evidence, not decoration: the witness proving it must be able to vouch for it under cross-examination, and the petition should candidly account for all accommodation available to the landlord, since Section 14(1)(e) turns on the absence of reasonably suitable alternatives. For tenants, the path to leave to defend and beyond runs through precise cross-examination — a bald denial of the site plan achieves nothing, but a concession extracted from the landlord's own witness can unravel the decree. And for both sides, a remand is not a result: the requirement question remains fully open before the Controller, now on a six-month clock.</p>
<div class="note">The High Court expressed no opinion on the merits of the landlords' requirement. The eviction petition of 2016 vintage now returns for a fresh look — a sobering illustration of how long rent control litigation can run when the first adjudication is incomplete.</div>
